From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 10 Sep 2026 12:42:55 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 87/91] test(witness): cover the interrupted reap and the
 residue it strands (gcp-mves)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

RED first, per the bead's acceptance criteria. Seven cases that fail against
the current polecat-worktree-reap.sh for the reason the bead names, not for a
syntax error; the fix lands in the next commit.

The reaper runs as the witness pre_start and a pre_start is SIGKILLed at
[session] setup_timeout. `git worktree remove` drops the worktree's .git file
and git's admin entry BEFORE it unlinks the tree, so a kill in that window
leaves a directory that is present on disk, has no .git, has no admin entry,
and produced no log line — the report is downstream of the kill. Gate 1
enumerates from `git worktree list`, which cannot see such a directory, so the
reaper can never look at it again. winnow lost 40 worktrees this way and
reported none of them (winnow-h0n2o).

No amount of dry-run soak could have caught it, which is why the cases are
written the way they are: a dry run never calls `git worktree remove` at all,
so the whole failure mode is outside what the staged rollout can observe. The
fixture therefore drives the destructive path directly — it stalls `git
worktree remove` half done (the .git file and admin entry already gone, the
tree still there) and SIGKILLs the reaper inside that window, which reproduces
the observed artifact exactly.

Cases: an interrupted removal leaves a marked, resumable state; no unmarked
half-removed directory survives any interruption; legacy residue is enumerated
by walking the disk rather than the admin list; residue still honours the
gates (one case per gate); residue with no bead is kept; the gates are
re-checked at the point of use; and the promotion criterion says what a
dry-run soak can and cannot demonstrate.

Two test hooks are new because the states they drive are not otherwise
observable from outside the script: GIT_REMOVE_HALFWAY_STALL /
GIT_LATER_PRUNE_DELAY hold the process inside either removal sequence so the
kill can land there, and GIT_DIRTY_AFTER_STATUS dirties a worktree the instant
gate 3 answers clean, which is a gate going stale between its check and the
removal it authorised. kill_after is hand-rolled rather than coreutils
`timeout`, for the same reason the script's own run_bounded carries a
fallback: coreutils is not on a stock macOS.

Validation: all seven fail against the unchanged script with the expected
messages (pasted verbatim into a bead note); the seventeen existing cases and
test_agent_pre_start_budget.sh stay green.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
